    historyContent: {
      origins: "Originates in 18th-century Russia, predating the modern standardized 'Podkidnoy Durak'. It belongs to the 'Nakladka' branch of games which emphasizes the volume of cards in the play pile.",
      originCountries: [
        'Russia',
      ],
      timeline: [
        "1700s: Early 'Nakladka' variants appear in Russian taverns.",
        "1800s: 'Kryt'-Navalivat' becomes a popular informal street game.",
        'Present: Occasionally played as a nostalgic or simpler alternative to modern Durak.',
      ],
      evolution: "Unlike modern Durak where the defender wants to clear the table, in Kryt'-Navalivat', the goal is to force the opponent into a massive 'pickup' by making the pile too difficult to beat.",
      cultural: "The game's name itself describes the rhythmic physical action of slapping cards down quickly on top of each other.",
